          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I get migraines about eight times a month. Is that the level where guidance describes prevention, and what lifestyle changes are described?",
          "answer": "Based on the guidance retrieved, yes, eight attacks a month is above the level where prevention is discussed. The national headache guidance (guideline-migraine-acute-and-preventive-treatment) says preventive treatment is offered when there are four or more migraine days per month **with some disability**. Fewer attacks can also qualify if they are severe, if acute treatment does not work or cannot be used, or if the person prefers prevention. Whether you meet the threshold depends partly on how much the attacks affect your daily life, which is a question for a clinician.\n\nThe guidance also says prevention aims to reduce how often and how severely attacks happen, not to stop them completely. A 50 percent reduction in migraine days is the usual measure of a good response.\n\nOn lifestyle, the search found one passage specific to migraine. The sleep and migraine summary (lifestyle-migraine-sleep) says that a steady daily routine helps. Sudden changes in sleep or daily habits are the changes most often linked with attacks. The other lifestyle passages retrieved were about diabetes and anxiety, so I have not used them here.\n\nThe retrieved material does not cover other lifestyle factors for migraine, such as diet, exercise, or stress. It also does not say what preventive treatments are used. Those are questions to take to your clinician. The search also did not find any warning signs that would need urgent care in what you described. If your attacks change in pattern or severity, or you develop new symptoms, seek medical advice promptly.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
